, safety, and professional practice\n\nEntertainment work also carries obligations beyond the finished artefact. Risks can include venue safety, harassment, recording rights, heckler escalation, and ownership of material. Strong production practice documents ownership, permissions, credits, approvals, and safety responsibilities early enough that they can shape decisions rather than become emergencies.\n\nAccessibility belongs in the same conversation. Captions, audio description, readable graphics, navigable digital interfaces, venue access, and other accommodations can expand who is able to experience the work and can improve clarity for wider audiences.\n\n## What to remember\n\nStand-up performance becomes easier to understand when craft, production systems, audience experience, rights, and safety are treated as connected. The most durable creative organisations learn from each project, document what worked, and improve the process as deliberately as they improve the final work.\n\nThis is an evergreen Blogdel entertainment explainer. Exact rules, contracts, technical standards, and platform requirements can change, so current primary documentation should be checked when a real production decision depends on exact wording.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/entertainment-fallback-2.png","featured_image_alt":"Stand-up performance: What beginners often misunderstand","word_count":643,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["entertainment","evergreen","explainer","Stand-up performance"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"WIPO","title":"WIPO","url":"https://www.wipo.int/copyright/en/","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"UNESCO","title":"UNESCO Culture","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/culture","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"WIPO","title":"World Intellectual Property Organization","url":"https://www.wipo.int/copyright/en/","authority":"primary","position":2}]},{"external_id":"entertainment-archive-2026-174","published_at":"2026-07-20T12:00:00.000Z","slug":"stand-up-performance-how-production-evolves-over-time-174","title":"Stand-up performance: How production evolves over time","description":"Stand-up performance explained through how production evolves over time, with craft, production, evaluation, rights, safety, and audience context.","body_markdown":"# Stand-up performance: How production evolves over time\n\nStand-up performance sits inside a larger entertainment system of creative intent, production craft, collaboration, technology, rights, distribution, and audience response. This article focuses on how production evolves over time while keeping those connections visible.\n\n## The core idea\n\nDelivering authored comic material live through timing, persona, audience reading, structure, and improvisation. Professional work rarely comes from one isolated decision. It emerges from a chain of choices that need to remain coherent as the project moves between people, tools, locations, and deadlines.\n\nThe practical vocabulary of stand-up performance includes writing, rehearsal, timing, microphone technique, crowd awareness, transitions, callbacks, improvisation, and set construction. These elements are useful because they let collaborators discuss the work precisely instead of relying only on taste or intuition.\n\n## How production evolves over time\n\nProduction evolves by moving from broad choices to increasingly specific ones. Early work defines intention and constraints, middle stages create and test material, and later stages refine continuity, quality, delivery, and audience fit. In stand-up performance, experienced teams also build feedback loops so mistakes discovered late become better planning decisions on the next project.\n\n## Creative intention meets production reality\n\nEntertainment production is a negotiation between ambition and constraints. Time, budget, access, performer availability, technology, rights, platform requirements, and audience expectations can all change what is possible. Constraints do not automatically reduce quality. Often they force a project to decide what matters most.\n\nThe strongest teams make those trade-offs explicit. They distinguish decisions that define the identity of the work from decisions that can change without damaging it. That distinction helps a production protect its central idea while still adapting to technical problems, schedule pressure, feedback, or distribution needs.\n\n## Collaboration and iteration\n\nCreative work becomes more reliable when feedback is attached to a clear objective. A note such as \"make it better\" is difficult to act on. A note about pacing, clarity, continuity, tone, audience understanding, technical quality, or a specific rights problem can be tested.\n\nIteration also changes with production stage. Early revisions are cheap and broad. Late revisions are usually narrower because more departments and assets depend on earlier choices. This is why experienced productions try to identify structural problems before expensive finishing work begins.\n\n## Evaluating the result\n\nFor stand-up performance, useful evaluation criteria include laugh response, pacing, command of the room, clarity, recovery, material consistency, and audience fit. These criteria do not replace artistic judgement. They make judgement more accountable by asking what the work is trying to achieve and whether the production choices support that aim.\n\nAudience response can add another layer of evidence, but popularity and quality are not identical. A project can be technically accomplished and reach a small audience, or attract a large audience while still containing craft problems. Good evaluation separates commercial outcomes, audience outcomes, and creative outcomes instead of treating them as one score.\n\n## Rights, safety, and professional practice\n\nEntertainment work also carries obligations beyond the finished artefact. Risks can include venue safety, harassment, recording rights, heckler escalation, and ownership of material. Strong production practice documents ownership, permissions, credits, approvals, and safety responsibilities early enough that they can shape decisions rather than become emergencies.\n\nAccessibility belongs in the same conversation. Captions, audio description, readable graphics, navigable digital interfaces, venue access, and other accommodations can expand who is able to experience the work and can improve clarity for wider audiences.\n\n## What to remember\n\nStand-up performance becomes easier to understand when craft, production systems, audience experience, rights, and safety are treated as connected. The most durable creative organisations learn from each project, document what worked, and improve the process as deliberately as they improve the final work.\n\nThis is an evergreen Blogdel entertainment explainer. Exact rules, contracts, technical standards, and platform requirements can change, so current primary documentation should be checked when a real production decision depends on exact wording.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/entertainment-fallback-3.png","featured_image_alt":"Stand-up performance: How production evolves over time","word_count":646,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["entertainment","evergreen","explainer","Stand-up performance"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"WIPO","title":"WIPO","url":"https://www.wipo.int/copyright/en/","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"UNESCO","title":"UNESCO Culture","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/culture","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"WIPO","title":"World Intellectual Property Organization","url":"https://www.wipo.int/copyright/en/","authority":"primary","position":2}]},{"external_id":"entertainment-archive-2026-175","published_at":"2026-07-21T12:00:00.000Z","slug":"stand-up-performance-what-rights-and-safety-standards-protect-175","title":"Stand-up performance: What rights and safety standards protect","description":"Stand-up performance explained through what rights and safety standards protect, with craft, production, evaluation, rights, safety, and audience context.","body_markdown":"# Stand-up performance: What rights and safety standards protect\n\nStand-up performance sits inside a larger entertainment system of creative intent, production craft, collaboration, technology, rights, distribution, and audience response. This article focuses on what rights and safety standards protect while keeping those connections visible.\n\n## The core idea\n\nDelivering authored comic material live through timing, persona, audience reading, structure, and improvisation. Professional work rarely comes from one isolated decision. It emerges from a chain of choices that need to remain coherent as the project moves between people, tools, locations, and deadlines.\n\nThe practical vocabulary of stand-up performance includes writing, rehearsal, timing, microphone technique, crowd awareness, transitions, callbacks, improvisation, and set construction. These elements are useful because they let collaborators discuss the work precisely instead of relying only on taste or intuition.\n\n## What rights and safety standards protect\n\nRights and safety standards protect both people and the creative work. Important risks in stand-up performance include venue safety, harassment, recording rights, heckler escalation, and ownership of material. Good production practice therefore treats contracts, permissions, credits, consent, workplace safety, accessibility, and documented approvals as part of the creative process rather than paperwork added at the end.\n\n## Creative intention meets production reality\n\nEntertainment production is a negotiation between ambition and constraints. Time, budget, access, performer availability, technology, rights, platform requirements, and audience expectations can all change what is possible. Constraints do not automatically reduce quality. Often they force a project to decide what matters most.\n\nThe strongest teams make those trade-offs explicit. They distinguish decisions that define the identity of the work from decisions that can change without damaging it. That distinction helps a production protect its central idea while still adapting to technical problems, schedule pressure, feedback, or distribution needs.\n\n## Collaboration and iteration\n\nCreative work becomes more reliable when feedback is attached to a clear objective. A note such as \"make it better\" is difficult to act on. A note about pacing, clarity, continuity, tone, audience understanding, technical quality, or a specific rights problem can be tested.\n\nIteration also changes with production stage. Early revisions are cheap and broad. Late revisions are usually narrower because more departments and assets depend on earlier choices. This is why experienced productions try to identify structural problems before expensive finishing work begins.\n\n## Evaluating the result\n\nFor stand-up performance, useful evaluation criteria include laugh response, pacing, command of the room, clarity, recovery, material consistency, and audience fit. These criteria do not replace artistic judgement. They make judgement more accountable by asking what the work is trying to achieve and whether the production choices support that aim.\n\nAudience response can add another layer of evidence, but popularity and quality are not identical. A project can be technically accomplished and reach a small audience, or attract a large audience while still containing craft problems. Good evaluation separates commercial outcomes, audience outcomes, and creative outcomes instead of treating them as one score.\n\n## Rights, safety, and professional practice\n\nEntertainment work also carries obligations beyond the finished artefact. Risks can include venue safety, harassment, recording rights, heckler escalation, and ownership of material. Strong production practice documents ownership, permissions, credits, approvals, and safety responsibilities early enough that they can shape decisions rather than become emergencies.\n\nAccessibility belongs in the same conversation. Captions, audio description, readable graphics, navigable digital interfaces, venue access, and other accommodations can expand who is able to experience the work and can improve clarity for wider audiences.\n\n## What to remember\n\nStand-up performance becomes easier to understand when craft, production systems, audience experience, rights, and safety are treated as connected. The most durable creative organisations learn from each project, document what worked, and improve the process as deliberately as they improve the final work.\n\nThis is an evergreen Blogdel entertainment explainer. Exact rules, contracts, technical standards, and platform requirements can change, so current primary documentation should be checked when a real production decision depends on exact wording.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/entertainment-fallback-2.png","featured_image_alt":"Stand-up performance: What rights and safety standards protect","word_count":651,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["entertainment","evergreen","explainer","Stand-up performance"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"WIPO","title":"WIPO","url":"https://www.wipo.int/copyright/en/","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"UNESCO","title":"UNESCO Culture","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/culture","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"WIPO","title":"World Intellectual Property Organization","url":"https://www.wipo.int/copyright/en/","authority":"primary","position":2}]}]$blogdel$::jsonb) as p(
  external_id text, published_at timestamptz, slug text, title text,
  description text, body_markdown text, article_type text, language text,
  featured_image_url text, featured_image_alt text, word_count integer,
  reading_time_minutes integer, keywords text[], provider text, model text,
  image_source_type text, image_provider text, image_model text, refs jsonb
);

insert into public.source_items (
  source_id, category_id, external_id, prompt, context, refs, instructions, status, source_published_at
)
select
  s.id, c.id, p.external_id,
  'Canonical GPT-5.6 Sol Entertainment archive article: ' || p.title,
  jsonb_build_object('headline',p.title,'published_at',p.published_at),
  p.refs,
  jsonb_build_object(
    'article_type','explainer',
    'tone','clear',
    'freshness','evergreen',
    'required_author_provider','chatgpt',
    'required_author_model','gpt-5.6-sol',
    'benchmark_cohort','sol-historical-archive-v1',
    'forbid_blogdel_text_generation',true,
    'image_policy','unique-first-fallback-allowed'
  ),
  'processed'::public.source_item_status,
  p.published_at
from _blogdel_ent_payload p
join public.categories c on c.slug='entertainment'
join public.sources s on s.category_id=c.id and s.slug='culture-media-evergreen' and s.is_enabled
where not exists (select 1 from public.source_items si where si.external_id=p.external_id);

update public.source_items si
set status='processed',
    rejection_reason=null,
    source_published_at=p.published_at,
    context=jsonb_build_object('headline',p.title,'published_at',p.published_at),
    refs=p.refs,
    instructions=coalesce(si.instructions,'{}'::jsonb) || jsonb_build_object(
      'required_author_provider','chatgpt',
      'required_author_model','gpt-5.6-sol',
      'benchmark_cohort','sol-historical-archive-v1',
      'forbid_blogdel_text_generation',true,
      'image_policy','unique-first-fallback-allowed'
    )
from _blogdel_ent_payload p
where si.external_id=p.external_id;

update public.articles a
set slug=p.slug,title=p.title,description=p.description,body_markdown=p.body_markdown,
    article_type=p.article_type::public.article_type_enum,language=p.language,
    status='published'::public.article_status_enum,featured_image_url=p.featured_image_url,
    featured_image_alt=p.featured_image_alt,word_count=p.word_count,
    reading_time_minutes=p.reading_time_minutes,keywords=p.keywords,
    provider='chatgpt',model='gpt-5.6-sol',is_demo=false,event_at=p.published_at,
    published_at=p.published_at,archived_at=null,generation_job_id=null,
    image_source_type=p.image_source_type,image_provider=p.image_provider,
    image_model=p.image_model,updated_at=now()
from _blogdel_ent_payload p
join public.source_items si on si.external_id=p.external_id
where a.source_item_id=si.id;

insert into public.articles (
  source_item_id,category_id,author_id,generation_job_id,slug,title,description,
  body_markdown,article_type,language,status,featured_image_url,featured_image_alt,
  word_count,reading_time_minutes,keywords,provider,model,is_demo,event_at,
  published_at,image_source_type,image_provider,image_model
)
select
  si.id,si.category_id,au.id,null,p.slug,p.title,p.description,p.body_markdown,
  p.article_type::public.article_type_enum,p.language,'published'::public.article_status_enum,
  p.featured_image_url,p.featured_image_alt,p.word_count,p.reading_time_minutes,p.keywords,
  'chatgpt','gpt-5.6-sol',false,p.published_at,p.published_at,
  p.image_source_type,p.image_provider,p.image_model
from _blogdel_ent_payload p
join public.source_items si on si.external_id=p.external_id
join lateral (
  select id from public.authors where category_id=si.category_id and is_active
  order by last_used_at asc nulls first limit 1
) au on true
where not exists (select 1 from public.articles a where a.source_item_id=si.id);

delete from public.article_references ar
using public.articles a, public.source_items si, _blogdel_ent_payload p
where ar.article_id=a.id and a.source_item_id=si.id and si.external_id=p.external_id;

insert into public.article_references (
  article_id,source_item_id,provider,title,url,authority,position,retrieved_at
)
select a.id,si.id,ref->>'provider',ref->>'title',ref->>'url',
       nullif(ref->>'authority',''),
       coalesce((ref->>'position')::integer,ordinality::integer-1),now()
from _blogdel_ent_payload p
join public.source_items si on si.external_id=p.external_id
join public.articles a on a.source_item_id=si.id
cross join lateral jsonb_array_elements(p.refs) with ordinality as r(ref,ordinality);

commit;
